\label{sec:signal_failure}

\paragraph{Min-K\%++ directional reversal.}
Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items, yielding Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005 and $d = -1.98$ at d100 relative to d000. A practitioner applying Min-K\%++ at this scale would conclude that contaminated items are \textit{less} likely to be memorized. At 1.5B, discrimination turns mildly positive ($d \approx +0.3$); at 3B, moderately positive ($d \approx +0.7$). We hypothesize that SFT injection at 0.5B disrupts the token-level surprisal landscape so severely that contaminated items (overfit at the response level) paradoxically show reduced token-level fluency.

\paragraph{Signal reliability across scales.}
Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} summarizes detection AUC across signal-scale-dosage combinations. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at d005 and above for all scales. S4 (Self-Critique) performs near-random at 0.5B and reaches marginal discrimination at 1.5B+. RRF fusion degrades to S1-only performance because S2 and S4 contribute noise at small scales.

Cross-family analysis with Llama-3.2-3B confirms that RRF exposure scores generalize across architectures, though pooled $\gamma$ loses diagnostic specificity (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_signal_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Detection signal AUC across scale, dosage, and signal type. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $>0.95$ at all scales; S2 (Min-K\%++) reverses at 0.5B; S4 (Self-Critique) is near-random below 1.5B.}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
\label{sec:identifiability}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_identifiability_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{$\gamma$ recovery as a function of signal AUC and ensemble size $J$. Bias $<0.05$ and 95\% CI coverage $\geq90\%$ require $J\geq50$ at AUC$=$0.55, but only $J\geq16$ at AUC$\geq$0.90.}
\label{fig:identifiability_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The simulation study (20 conditions, 10 seeds each, 200 runs) establishes the operating envelope. Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} presents bias and CI coverage as a function of signal quality and ensemble size. Recovery succeeds (bias $< 0.05$, 95\% CI coverage $\geq 90\%$) at AUC as low as 0.55 with $J \geq 50$. Posterior CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$, close to the parametric $J^{-0.5}$ rate. Current IRT-based LLM evaluation operates at $J = 8$--$30$ \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}, well below this threshold. Signal quality is the binding constraint: when AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), even $J = 16$ suffices. Our $J = 13$ results confirm the analysis: calibration via $\theta$ works, but per-domain $\gamma$ remains exploratory.

